LinkedList<E> implements List<E>, Deque<E>, Queue<E>

So LinkedList is:
  A List (indexed operations)
  A Queue (FIFO)
  A Deque (double-ended queue)

LinkedList using Queue interface methods: (insert last , remove first)
  Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>(); // FIFO
  q.offer(1); // add to end
  q.poll(); // remove from front
  q.peek(); // look at front without removing

Why LinkedList Is Rarely Used as Queue in Production

Although it works, in real systems:
   LinkedList is usually NOT preferred.
Reasons:
   ❌ 1. High Memory Overhead
            Each element = object + 2 pointers
   ❌ 2. Poor Cache Locality
            Slower in practice
   ❌ 3. Not Thread-Safe
            Needs external synchronization
   ❌ 4. No Capacity Control

LinkedList using Deque interface methods: (insert/remove at both ends)
  Deque<Integer> deque = new LinkedList<>();
  deque.offerFirst(1); // add to front
  deque.offerLast(2); // add to end
  deque.pollFirst(); // remove from front
  deque.pollLast(); // remove from end

